## Electrical envelope

### [CXC-R503-PINOUT] Six pins on a captive pigtail

- pins: 1 VCC (DC 3.3 V), 2 GND, 3 TXD, 4 RXD, 5 WAKEUP (standby high, finger low), 6 3.3VT (touch supply, DC 3-5 V) — p.3.
- pin-1-evidence: identified only by a photograph. No keying drawing, no polarity mark.
- rule: never infer pinout from wire colour. The manual disclaims it outright: the line order has nothing to do with color (p.3).
- verify-by-continuity: [CXC-R503-EV-01] meters each contact to a known net before any power is applied.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-RAILS] Two independent rails, an unregulated input, and the power topology ruling

- vcc: pin 1, DC 3.3 V, a point value with no tolerance band anywhere in any revision.
- vt: pin 6, DC 3-5 V (V1.1 said 3-6 V; silently narrowed).
- rails-are-independent: the manual's own low-power mode is VCC off with 3.3VT on (p.43), which is the ground of the WAKEUP inference below.
- input-is-unregulated: power is supplied directly to the image sensor and decoding chip (p.4); ripple recommendation 50 mV peak-to-peak; board-side filtering is the host's job.
- no-hot-plug: connecting or removing the cable live may damage the module (p.4).
- power-cycle-dwell: after power-off, wait at least two seconds before power-on (p.4).
- ruling-share-the-ldo: David, 2026-08-17 — slave the R503 to the ESP32's 3.3 V LDO; he has done this pairing before with no problem. No separate regulator.
- ruling-no-power-gating: David, 2026-08-17 — no programmatic VCC switching. SoftRst 0x3D covers reset; the build is USB-powered; not gating VCC retires the two-second dwell and the missing inrush figure outright. Reopen only if a battery-powered device is specified.
- ruling-power-optimization-later: David, 2026-08-17 — even a battery device is built first with no power optimization; optimization is a named second pass against a working build. No high-side switch, no VCC-off pattern, no duty cycling in a first build.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-WAKEUP-RAIL] WAKEUP's high level

- claim: WAKEUP's high level tracks the 3.3VT rail, not VCC.
- evidence-class: INFERENCE, not a datasheet fact. The manual says only that the touch output is CMOS and roughly the input voltage (p.3), inside a paragraph about the touch supply; and the low-power mode requires WAKEUP to work while VCC is off, which is only possible if it is powered from 3.3VT.
- rule: tie 3.3VT to 3.3 V unless and until measured otherwise. At 3.3VT = 5 V the inference puts roughly 5 V on WAKEUP, which overdrives a non-5V-tolerant ESP32 GPIO.
- corollary-rule: drive RXD only from a 3.3 V pin. No revision states any 5 V tolerance for any input, and no logic-level figures exist at all — no VIH, VIL, VOH, VOL, drive strength or pull-up requirement anywhere ([CXC-R503-EV-03] measures the safe case; no overdrive test is authored, because deliberately overdriving an input is a brick-risk test and none survives in this spec).
- vendor-precedent: the vendor's own reference circuit does not wire TOUCH to a GPIO; it buffers it into a power-latch path. That circuit is drawn for the 5 V R307 and its rail must not be copied — building it as drawn puts 5 V on an R503's pin 1 (p.44).
- tested-by: [CXC-R503-EV-03], [CXC-R503-EV-12] (the second is the negative control)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-CURRENT] The ring LED's cost is not determinable from the document

- working-current: 20 mA during acquisition (p.1).
- standby-current: about 2 uA with VCC off and 3.3VT on (p.1, p.3).
- absent: no idle figure, no peak or inrush figure, and no ring-LED figure of any kind, in any revision — established by search, not inference.
- consequence: whether 20 mA includes the ring is unknown, and the ring runs for the whole enrollment ceremony. [CXC-R503-EV-04] measures the three states the manual conflates, including the ring at full white, and sizes the shared-LDO decision with data.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-CONNECTOR] The connector question was dissolved, not answered

- ruling: David, 2026-08-17 — the JST connector is cut off and the six conductors are soldered direct to a prototyping board. There is no mating half, so no footprint, so the MX1.0 versus SH1.0 designation contradiction has nothing to attach to.
- preserved-references: one module retains its connector intact, and the severed connector is kept with its wire stubs as the wire-order reference for the cut module — physical negative controls, both David's own.
- if-a-production-board-ever-carries-a-socket: the pitch question returns unchanged (three revisions say MX1.0--6P, V1.4.1 says SH1.0--6P, and the two project sources disagree on what MX1.0 denotes). A caliper on the reference module settles it; no document can.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

