## Wire protocol

### [CXC-R503-FRAMING] The packet

- shape: header 2B 0xEF01, address 4B, PID 1B, length 2B, content, checksum 2B (p.8).
- byte-order: big-endian throughout. The statement is absent from V1.1, so a V1.1-era driver had no byte-order statement at all.
- address-gating: default 0xFFFFFFFF; a wrong address produces SILENCE, not an error packet.
- length-semantics: length counts the content PLUS the two checksum bytes. A parser reading it as content length overruns by exactly two bytes on every packet.
- pid-values: 01 command, 02 data, 07 acknowledge, 08 end-of-data.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-CHECKSUM] The checksum rule, verified

- rule: arithmetic sum of PID, both length bytes and all content bytes, modulo 2^16. Header and address are NOT included.
- verification: recomputed against all 21 worked examples across the document set; all 21 recompute exactly. This is the only claim in the R503 corpus carrying a passed falsifiable test rather than a citation.
- obligation: every packet's checksum is verified before its payload is acted on — containment invariant inv-5 in [CXC-FPHAL-CONTAINMENT-INVARIANTS]. One surveyed driver skips verification and says so in a comment.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-PACKET-256] The 256-byte packet case never worked

- contradiction: the stated 256-byte maximum packet length forbids the 256-byte data payload the system parameter permits (length would be 258). Only the 128-byte case is ever worked in any revision.
- rule: do not size a buffer or a state machine on the assumption that 256-byte data packets are legal. The caller's buffer is the binding check ([CXC-FPHAL-CONTAINMENT-INVARIANTS] inv-2).
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-OPCODES] The command set, with the corrections deployed drivers get wrong

- core-set: GetImg 0x01, GenChar 0x02, Match 0x03, Search 0x04, RegModel 0x05, Store 0x06, LoadChar 0x07, UpChar 0x08, DownChar 0x09, DeletChar 0x0C, Empty 0x0D, SetSysPara 0x0E, ReadSysPara 0x0F, TempleteNum 0x1D, ReadIndexTable 0x1F, GetImageEx 0x28, Cancel 0x30, AutoEnroll 0x31, AutoIdentify 0x32, AuraLedConfig 0x35, CheckSensor 0x36, GetAlgVer 0x39, GetFwVer 0x3A, ReadProdInfo 0x3C, SoftRst 0x3D, HandShake 0x40, plus notepad and password commands.
- correction-handshake: HandShake is 0x40. Two of six surveyed drivers ship 0x53.
- correction-nonexistent: 0x1B HiSpeedSearch does not exist on the R503; nor do 0x33 or 0x50 through 0x53. Legacy opcodes count as out-of-spec, not coverage.
- correction-removed: 0x17 PortControl existed in V1.1 only; whether firmware still honours it is unknown.
- readprodinfo-length-contradiction: the ack length implies 46 data bytes while the field rows sum to 50; V1.2.1 was self-consistent at 46. A driver must be told which to expect; [CXC-R503-EV-10] counts the bytes on the wire.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-CODES] Confirmation codes, and the one the firmware uses that no table documents

- documented: 35 codes in V1.4.1 (pp.9-10). Load-bearing ones: 00 complete, 01 receive error, 02 no finger, 09 no match, 1F library full, 26 timeout, 27 already exists, FC unsupported, FD hardware error, FE execution failure.
- undocumented-in-use: 0x28 is returned by the vendor's own worked example (p.38) and appears in no revision's table. An undefined registration-logic setting is invoked in the same paragraphs and defined nowhere.
- rule: an unlisted confirmation code is surfaced, never fatal, and never mapped onto the nearest documented one — [CXC-FPHAL-UNKNOWN-CODE-HANDLING].
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-UNSOLICITED-BYTE] The bare 0x55, at power-on and after every soft reset

- behaviour: the module emits a single unframed byte 0x55 unsolicited — at power-on and again after every SoftRst 0x3D. It is the only unsolicited emission in the protocol.
- hazard: a parser requiring the 0xEF01 header sees one byte of garbage; a fixed-frame parser is permanently phase-shifted. A driver that handles it only at boot desyncs on its first soft reset.
- rule: drain RX immediately before every transmit AND parse with a sliding two-byte header window. Either alone is partial; no surveyed driver has both. This is containment invariant inv-4.
- tested-by: [CXC-R503-EV-05], [CXC-R503-EV-06] (power-on capture; re-emission after SoftRst)
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-ACK-STREAM] The AutoEnroll acknowledge stream

- behaviour: with its status bit set, one AutoEnroll 0x31 command emits FIFTEEN acknowledge packets, steps 0x01 through 0x0F; only the final packet's fingerID field is meaningful.
- config-index-trap: the enabling parameter is Config3 on AutoEnroll and Config1 on AutoIdentify — a driver that generalises the index silently misconfigures the sibling command.
- timeout-contradiction: the no-finger exit is 10 s in prose and 15 s in the flowchart, for both commands, in three revisions. The implementation takes the reading that fails later and records the other value ([CXC-FPHAL-CONTAINMENT-INVARIANTS] inv-7).
- version-trap: V1.2.1's step codes are numerically incoherent and were fixed in V1.4; a driver written against V1.2.1's table mis-sequences on current firmware.
- fleet-status: the packaged AutoEnroll path is NOT USED, by ruling — it mandates its own on-module LED sequence, which is unimplementable under device-owned ring ([CXC-FPID-ENROLLMENT-PATH]). The stream facts are retained because the trap survives for any future reader who reaches for the packaged command.
- no-prior-art: not one of six surveyed open-source drivers implements AutoEnroll or AutoIdentify. The stream behaviour is untested by every available implementation.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-STREAM-BOUND] AutoIdentify's stream can be unbounded

- claim: with retries configured to 0 and a finger continuously present that never matches, AutoIdentify's acknowledge stream is unbounded. The no-finger timeout does not bound the finger-present-never-matches case.
- evidence-class: INFERENCE from the stated retry semantics; the manual illustrates only the 3-packet success case.
- consequence: a fixed-size RX ring on a CH32V203-class MCU is sized from a measurement, not from the success case. [CXC-R503-EV-07] is the falsifier.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-DATA-BURSTS] Data-packet bursts

- behaviour: UpImage, DownImage, UpChar, DownChar and ReadInfPage return an acknowledge then several data packets. The packet COUNT depends on the negotiated packet-size parameter, not on the command; a driver terminates on the 0x08 end-of-data PID or reads the configured size first.
- driver-hazard: one surveyed driver scans a fixed buffer for 0xEF01 boundaries, which mis-splits on payload bytes that happen to be 0xEF01; another has no data-packet support at all.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-BUSY] Behaviour while Busy is undefined

- what-exists: a Busy flag (system status bit 0, via ReadSysPara) and Cancel 0x30.
- what-is-missing: what happens when a command arrives while Busy — queued, rejected or dropped — is addressed nowhere in any revision. Three different transaction layers hang off the answer.
- tested-by: [CXC-R503-EV-08]
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

### [CXC-R503-BAUD] The baud contradiction, and the rule that removes the risk

- contradiction: every revision carries three mutually contradictory statements of legal baud rates — an N=1..6 table, an N in {1,2,4,6,12} enumeration, and a 9600-115200 range sentence. One widely-used driver faithfully implements the range sentence and ships seven forbidden constants.
- brick-risk: setting a forbidden rate and power-cycling could leave the module at an unreachable baud, and no recovery path is documented anywhere. A power cycle does not recover it.
- rule: the implementation NEVER issues a baud change. It binds the documented default of 57600 and leaves it — containment invariant inv-8. With that rule in force the risk cannot arise, and no baud-probing test is authored anywhere in this corpus.
- status: draft
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-R503]

